I’ll first inspect the measurement metadata, pattern, and reusable analysis scripts, then establish a physically constrained baseline before testing phase candidates. I’ll keep candidate rejection separate from chemistry plausibility and preserve the exact final GSAS-II project behind the exported CIFs and profile.

The chemistry is a Mn–W–O solid-state reaction at 700 °C with Cu Kα1/Kα2 explicitly present and no calibrated profile. I’m now extracting the peak families and querying exact-composition records first; likely phases will remain provisional until each is challenged against unique reflections under one shared profile.

The peak inventory now strongly favors wolframite-type MnWO₄: the split ±hkl families, including 29.58/30.03°, are indexed coherently by monoclinic P2/c. A physically valid but older FeWO₄ prototype creates a strong unobserved 40.42° family; ZnWO₄ and MgWO₄ parents suppress it and reproduce the observed 40.08/40.76° pair, so I’m carrying the Zn-based Mn-substituted model forward and keeping Mn oxides, WO₃, and Mn₃WO₆ on the residual watch list.

The shared-profile replay favors a single specimen-displacement correction over a constant zero, though only marginally by Rwp (13.280% versus 13.291%). More importantly, the accepted baseline now has nonnegative W/X terms and neutral fixed phase size/strain; the earlier negative U was traced to GSAS-II’s nonzero default microstrain, not evidence for an exotic profile. I’m inspecting the residual families next before allowing any structural-coordinate or texture terms.

The watch-list challenge is running each WO₃ polymorph and Mn-bearing alternative from the same saved one-phase baseline. Each candidate gets a scale-only test, then a cell/angular reconvergence and a rebuilt common profile; this prevents a weak candidate from “winning” merely by borrowing background or broadening terms.

The residual inventory identifies unreacted WO₃ decisively. Triclinic WO₃ lowers Rwp from 13.28% to 8.93% and its scale remains positive before any cell/profile release; it explains the independent 22.7°, 26.2°, 28.2°, 32.8/33.7°, 41.5°, and 46.8° families. Orthorhombic and high-pressure WO₃ alternatives fit substantially worse, while Mn₃WO₆ collapses to zero scale and Mn oxides provide only small, incoherent gains. I’m checking one additional physically sound ambient monoclinic WO₃ record before locking the polymorph.

The weak hausmannite phase survives the clean-profile re-challenge and full replay: multiple unique/partly overlapped families improve together, its refined cell remains plausible, and the three-phase fractions give an overall Mn:W ratio very close to 1:1. The provisional quantitative result is about 78.6 wt% MnWO₄, 15.6 wt% WO₃, and 5.75 wt% Mn₃O₄. I’m now testing only replay-stable structural/profile refinements before finalizing those values.

The phase inventory is now coherent: MnWO₄ is dominant, with residual WO₃ and a weak but repeatable Mn₃O₄ contribution. I’m finishing the replay and nuisance-term challenges now, then I’ll export every requested artifact directly from the reopened final GSAS-II project so the CIFs, fractions, and profile remain internally exact.

The structural refinement itself is behaving physically: refining the MnWO₄ oxygen coordinates lowers Rwp materially and regularizes both Mn–O and W–O octahedra, rather than producing short contacts. I’m now stress-testing that model from an independent wolframite coordinate start and testing size, strain, and polarization one at a time; unsupported terms will be pruned before the final replay.

The challenge suite is still running because each trial is being fully reconverged, not evaluated from a single linearized cycle. The independent MgWO₄-start replay has already passed without numerical failure; I’m waiting for the six phase-width trials to complete so I can distinguish genuine broadening from terms that merely run to neutral bounds.

These fits are slower than the baseline because GSAS-II is recalculating the full 10–100° pattern for every challenge. I’m letting them finish because the weak Mn₃O₄ phase is exactly where a one-cycle result could falsely suggest a finite crystallite-size or strain term.

The run is progressing normally but produces no console text because its output is being captured for side-by-side comparison. I’ll keep only a term that reaches a finite, replay-stable value and improves coherent peak families; otherwise the neutral common-profile model remains the submitted baseline.

The first challenge results are decisive in two places: the MnWO₄ coordinates replay from the MgWO₄ analogue to the same minimum, and polarization refines to a plausible 0.912 with a clear fit gain. The dominant phase’s size and strain collapse to neutral bounds. Residual WO₃ is genuinely broader; I’m now discriminating finite-size from strain broadening under the accepted polarization model, because those alternatives change its inferred fraction appreciably.

The broadening comparison is now testing three nested models—size only, strain only, and both—using identical background, polarization, cells, scales, and atomic model. This is the key guard against reporting a precise-looking WO₃ fraction that is actually conditional on an arbitrary peak-width surrogate.

The trial remains stable; it is simply compute-heavy over 8,300 points. Once these three models settle, I’ll use the simpler one unless the combined terms are both independently significant and improve different angular regimes.

No failure or bound warning has appeared. The last nested fit is the slowest because size and strain are correlated through peak breadth; that correlation itself is part of the model-selection evidence rather than something to hide in the final fraction.

Finite-size broadening is the supported WO₃ description: about 46 nm, Rwp 7.766%, versus 1.28% microstrain at Rwp 7.853%. Adding both does not find a better minimum and leaves the strain poorly determined, so strain is being rejected. The resulting fractions are stable between the two physical alternatives to roughly 0.3 percentage point; I’m doing the final profile-shape and minor-phase breadth checks against this cleaner baseline now.

The final profile suite is intentionally conservative: it compares Gaussian, Lorentzian, and refined mixing for the WO₃ size term, then re-tests only MnWO₄ and Mn₃O₄ breadth. No texture or anisotropic-width parameters are being added unless a simple isotropic term first survives this test.

The clean baseline remains numerically stable through the shape variants. The remaining time is in the two weak-phase width trials, where full replay is important because Mn₃O₄ contributes only a few percent and can otherwise trade scale against breadth.

So far there are no GSAS-II constraint or singularity warnings. I’m preserving that as a hard requirement: any apparent fit gain accompanied by a boundary solution or unstable minor-phase scale will be excluded from the submitted project.

The suite is on its final trial. After it completes, the remaining work is deterministic: one save/reopen replay, profile diagnostics at isolated low/mid/high-angle families, export from that reopened project, and consistency checks on every requested file.

The profile-shape test selects a pure Lorentzian WO₃ size term (75 ± 3 nm); allowing the mixing coefficient to refine drives it to that boundary, so I’m fixing it there and pruning the extra parameter. MnWO₄ again has no resolvable phase-specific breadth. Mn₃O₄ shows a possible finite microstrain term, but because it changes that weak phase from about 5.4 to 8.8 wt%, I’m treating it as a potential scale/background surrogate and will accept it only if the gain maps onto its unique peak families under the selected WO₃ profile.

The Mn₃O₄ strain challenge is being rejected. Its optimum implies FWHM values of roughly 0.9° near 32°, 2.5° near 59°, and over 4° above 70°; the gain is concentrated in broad low/mid-angle envelopes and the model worsens several high-angle Mn₃O₄ families. That is a scale/background surrogate, not a defensible crystallite parameter. I’ll submit the neutral-width Mn₃O₄ model and explicitly report its model-dependent fraction range.

The selected final model is now fixed conceptually: P2/c MnWO₄ with refined coordinates, P−1 WO₃ with one Lorentzian size term, and neutral-width I4₁/amd Mn₃O₄. I’m rebuilding it over the exact measured 9.903–100.095° range—the earlier development fits used 10–100°—then saving, reopening, and replaying all active parameters before any exports.

The full-range replay exposed an important structural uncertainty rather than a file problem: the broad WO₃ phase’s triclinic α and γ angles drift toward 90° by several formal standard uncertainties while Rwp changes only 0.002%. That invalidates the earlier “triclinic decisively preferred” conclusion under the cleaner broadening model. I’m re-challenging the simpler monoclinic WO₃ record under the exact same final nuisance model before locking the phase CIF.

The re-challenge changes the phase assignment in a useful way: monoclinic P2₁/c WO₃ (COD 2311041) is both simpler and better than the drifting triclinic model—Rwp 7.712% versus 7.741%—and replays with maximum shift/esd 0.003. A different monoclinic coordinate record gives 7.953%, confirming that the bad record, not monoclinic WO₃ itself, was the earlier rejection. I’m adopting the stable P2₁/c model and will keep its atomic coordinates fixed because their record dependence is not resolvable from these broad powder peaks.

The adopted project now replays cleanly from disk over all 8,301 points: Rwp 7.7118%, Rp 5.1349%, GOF 3.1028, and maximum shift/esd 0.0013. The final crystalline fractions are 74.83% MnWO₄, 19.67% monoclinic WO₃, and 5.50% Mn₃O₄; omitting Mn₃O₄ raises Rwp to 7.8809%, so its presence is supported even though its exact fraction remains model-sensitive.

The exported geometry is physical, but the profile inspection found a remaining baseline issue: several isolated MnWO₄ peaks are slightly narrower at low/mid angle than the current two-term W/X profile, while higher-angle breadth is not correspondingly narrow. I’m challenging one nonnegative U term now. This is the appropriate shared angle-dependent width correction; I won’t compensate it with phase texture or arbitrary phase broadening.

The U challenge collapses exactly to zero, so the W/X baseline is already the supported nonnegative profile. The remaining peak-center offsets form a real angular trend—about +0.04° at 15°, falling toward zero near 80°—and the current displacement is highly correlated with asymmetry. Per the baseline rule, I’m reopening the zero-versus-displacement choice on the final phase inventory before considering any anisotropy or texture.

The zero-correction replay is taking longer because the cells must re-equilibrate after removing the 787 µm displacement. The comparison is otherwise stable; I’m waiting for the cell/angle solution to stop moving so the decision is based on peak trends and geometry, not the first compensating cycle.

The angular-correction challenge is still converging without warnings. This is expected because zero and specimen displacement are strongly covariant with all three phase cells; I’m keeping the shared profile and structures otherwise identical.

This trial is unusually slow relative to the displacement replay, which itself is evidence of a more difficult covariance landscape. I’ll still let the prescribed eight full refinements finish, then check both convergence and the actual low-to-high-angle center residuals.

The process remains active but silent because results are captured atomically. If this zero model does not finish with a stable small shift/esd, it will fail the replay criterion regardless of its nominal Rwp.

The zero model is still consuming CPU rather than failing immediately. I’m allowing one more convergence interval; the existing displacement model is already stable to 0.0013 shift/esd, so the alternative must meet a comparable replay standard to replace it.

The zero alternative has now driven a cell/d-spacing calculation outside the physical arcsine domain after several minutes. That is a genuine replay failure, so I’m terminating this challenge and retaining the converged reflection-geometry displacement model. The remaining small center trend will be reported as an instrumental/systematic limitation, not absorbed into a second angular correction.

The displacement model remains the only replay-stable angular baseline. I’m now completing the required no-texture comparison against five symmetry-distinct MnWO₄ axes. Any accepted March–Dollase term must improve multiple isolated families and remain finite; a single-peak intensity correction will be rejected.

Texture is not being accepted: several inequivalent axes give nearly indistinguishable tiny gains (ΔRwp only 0.013–0.016%) with opposite March–Dollase directions, so there is no unique multi-family texture signal. I’m doing the last width check now—two-parameter uniaxial size and strain along the three monoclinic axes—because the isolated-family widths differ enough to warrant that explicit challenge.

The anisotropic strain trials are collapsing one or both components to zero, producing the expected divide-by-zero warnings in GSAS-II’s uniaxial formula. That already argues against strain; I’m allowing the bounded size trials to finish so the anisotropic-width decision covers both physical mechanisms.

Multiple crystallographic directions are independently reaching the same zero-strain singular limit, rather than a finite anisotropic solution. The size trials are bounded away from zero and will show whether any directional broadening survives without that numerical pathology.

The strain branch has conclusively failed: it repeatedly reaches exact zero components, propagating NaNs into derivatives and shift/esd. I’m stopping that failed suite now and will run the bounded anisotropic-size branch separately so a bad strain parameterization cannot contaminate it.

The isolated size-only suite is running normally with explicit 0.01–10 µm bounds. This will tell us whether the varying apparent widths have a reproducible crystallographic direction or are simply overlap/Kα-doublet effects.

The three directional-size fits remain stable but are compute-heavy because each adds two phase-width derivatives across all MnWO₄ reflections. I’ll reject any solution that parks either component at the 10 µm neutral bound or depends strongly on the chosen axis.

All optional-profile challenges are now resolved. U returns to zero; texture axes are ambiguous with only 0.013–0.016% Rwp gains; uniaxial strain collapses to singular zero values; and uniaxial size is bound-hit or replay-unstable with uncertainties larger than the parameters. The submitted neutral MnWO₄ profile is therefore the defensible model, with the residual low-angle center/shape trend carried into the uncertainty statement.

The requested files have now been generated directly from the reopened accepted project. I’m at the final integrity pass: cross-checking project fractions and cells against `result.json` and all three CIFs, verifying every `fit.csv` row equals the GSAS-II observed/calculated/residual arrays, and confirming no rejected refinement flag remains active.

Completed the phase analysis and full-range GSAS-II refinement.

